#N canvas 0 0 720 780 10;
#X text 12 8 sid~ - PSID (.sid) player that drives one sid6581~;
#X text 12 28 Loads a PSID file (v1-v4) and runs the tune's own 6502 player code through the in-house replay engine (memory map with VIC raster + CIA timer traps). Outlet 0 emits every $D400-$D418 write the tune makes as `reg <a> <d>` messages - connect it straight to sid6581~ \, which also receives `clock <Hz>` and `model <6581|8580>` on load so the chip configures itself (PAL/NTSC and the file's SID model).;
#X text 12 76 The tune controls its own tempo: VBI tunes play at the video frame rate (50 Hz PAL / 60 Hz NTSC) \, CIA tunes program their own timer - sid~ follows the latch the tune writes. RSID files (tunes that play through their own interrupt handlers) and MUS data are rejected: a basic replay cannot run them.;
#X msg 20 150 load demo.sid;
#X msg 120 150 play;
#X msg 180 150 pause;
#X msg 240 150 stop;
#X msg 300 150 info;
#X text 20 180 demo.sid ships with the examples - a hand-assembled CIA-timed A-minor loop (pulse lead \, saw bass \, noise drums). Any .sid on the pd search path or next to a patch works too.;
#X obj 20 240 sid~;
#X obj 20 290 sid6581~;
#X obj 20 340 *~ 0.3;
#X obj 20 390 dac~;
#X text 20 430 messages: load <file.sid> (stops + emits clock/model) \, play / pause / stop \, subtune <1..n> (multi-tune files \, reloads and re-inits) \, info (version \, tunes \, addresses \, clock \, model \, play rate \, state).;
#X text 20 480 reads the tune makes: $D419-$D41E (paddles \, OSC3 \, ENV3) go to the chip side when available \, OSC3-based random numbers are approximated. ROM pages hold RTS stubs (no C64 ROM ships with the app) \, and the VIC raster line advances with real time so raster-wait loops finish.;
#X connect 3 0 9 0;
#X connect 4 0 9 0;
#X connect 5 0 9 0;
#X connect 6 0 9 0;
#X connect 7 0 9 0;
#X connect 9 0 10 0;
#X connect 10 0 11 0;
